</span></div>
</figure>
<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.1 </span>Implementation Detail</h3>

<div id="S4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Training was conducted in two stages: pretraining on the cleaned LAION-CC-SBU-558k dataset, followed by visual instruction tuning on the cleaned LLaVA-v1.5-mix665k dataset. To demonstrate the benefits of <span id="S4.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Safe-LLaVA</span>, we pre-train and fine-tune LLaVAOneVision-0.5B and LLaVA-v1.5-7B models leading to <span id="S4.SS1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Safe-LLaVA (0.5B)</span> and <span id="S4.SS1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Safe-LLaVA (7B)</span> respectively.
We now focus on evaluating <span id="S4.SS1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Safe-LLaVA</span> models along with other leading MLLMs under the <span id="S4.SS1.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">PRISM-Small</span> benchmark using GPT-4 and Gemini-2.0-flash as evaluators. Results on <span id="S4.SS1.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">PRISM-Large</span> are reported in Supplementary Section <a href="#A3" title="Appendix C PRISM-Large Results ‣ Safe-LLaVA: A Privacy-Preserving Vision-Language Dataset and Benchmark for Biometric Safety" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C</span></a>. We also describe detailed environment and hyperparameters for both model training and testing in Supplementary Section <a href="#A2" title="Appendix B Implementation Details ‣ Safe-LLaVA: A Privacy-Preserving Vision-Language Dataset and Benchmark for Biometric Safety" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.2 </span>Results</h3>

<div id="S4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Table <a href="#S4.T2" title="Table 2 ‣ 4 Experiment ‣ Safe-LLaVA: A Privacy-Preserving Vision-Language Dataset and Benchmark for Biometric Safety" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> shows the results of implicit biometric information leakage under open-ended prompts. <span id="S4.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Safe-LLaVA</span> achieves the best leakage protection (the lowest leakage) across most attributes, demonstrating the effectiveness of the <span id="S4.SS2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Safe-LLaVA</span> dataset in preventing implicit privacy violations. Both <span id="S4.SS2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Safe-LLaVA</span> models significantly outperform other SoTA MLLMs in terms of implicit biometric information protection.
Especially, <span id="S4.SS2.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Safe-LLaVA (0.5B)</span> achieves an average leakage rate of <span id="S4.SS2.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">97.1%</span> under GPT-4 evaluation and <span id="S4.SS2.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">90.7%</span> under Gemini-2.0-flash, outperforming the base model by a margin of <span id="S4.SS2.p1.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">&gt;45%</span> on average. <span id="S4.SS2.p1.1.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Safe-LLaVA (7B)</span> also achieves similar performance gain on average with margin of <span id="S4.SS2.p1.1.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">47%</span>. Interestingly, <span id="S4.SS2.p1.1.10" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Safe-LLaVA (0.5B)</span> surpasses its larger counterpart, <span id="S4.SS2.p1.1.11" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Safe-LLaVA (7B)</span>, which slightly underperforms due to its tendency to generate longer and more descriptive responses, increasing the risk of unintended leakage.
The overall biometric privacy performances of state-of-the-art MLLMs on <span id="S4.SS2.p1.1.12" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">PRISM-Small</span> benchmark are summarized in Figure <a href="#S4.F6" title="Figure 6 ‣ 4.2 Results ‣ 4 Experiment ‣ Safe-LLaVA: A Privacy-Preserving Vision-Language Dataset and Benchmark for Biometric Safety" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Table <a href="#S4.T3" title="Table 3 ‣ 4 Experiment ‣ Safe-LLaVA: A Privacy-Preserving Vision-Language Dataset and Benchmark for Biometric Safety" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> presents the refusal accuracy across biometric attributes under both soft and hard prompts. <span id="S4.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Safe-LLaVA (0.5B &amp; 7B)</span> consistently outperforms all other models across categories, demonstrating the effectiveness of our <span id="S4.SS2.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Safe-LLaVA</span> dataset in explicitly teaching privacy-preserving refusal behavior. Notably, <span id="S4.SS2.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Safe-LLaVA (7B)</span> outperforms <span id="S4.SS2.p2.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Safe-LLaVA (0.5B)</span> in refusal accuracy, highlighting the benefit of a larger language backbone in learning to reject privacy-violating prompts more reliably.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F6" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.00192v1/images/Performance_visualization.png" id="S4.F6.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:452/134;" width="452" height="134" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 6: </span>Average refusal accuracy on various models (left) and implicit leakage protection score (right) on <span id="S4.F6.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">PRISM-Small</span> benchmark across MLLMs. <span id="S4.F6.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Safe-LLaVA</span>-0.5B model performs the best in terms of preserving biometric privacy.</figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="S4.F7" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.00192v1/images/Prompt_Visualization1.png" id="S4.F7.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/243;" width="476" height="243" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 7: </span>Qualitative examples of biometric information leakage on <span id="S4.F7.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">PRISM</span> benchmark across state-of-the-art MLLMs.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S4.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Finally, Figure <a href="#S4.F7" title="Figure 7 ‣ 4.2 Results ‣ 4 Experiment ‣ Safe-LLaVA: A Privacy-Preserving Vision-Language Dataset and Benchmark for Biometric Safety" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a> demonstrates that state-of-the-art MLLMs frequently infer sensitive biometric attributes including age, gender, race, and body weight; even when not explicitly required. These responses highlight how such models internalize and reproduce biased visual priors, raising privacy and fairness concerns in real-world deployment.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.3 </span>General Performance</h3>

<div id="S4.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To evaluate the semantic preservation, we assess model performance on several widely-used general-purpose LMM benchmarks e.g., SEED-Bench <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">29</a>]</cite>, AI2D <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">24</a>]</cite>, POPE <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">35</a>]</cite>, MMStar <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>]</cite>. These benchmarks primarily test general visual reasoning and understanding. We observe that <span id="S4.SS3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Safe-LLaVA</span> achieves performance that is comparable to or better than the base LLaVA across all benchmarks. As shown in Figure <a href="#S4.F8" title="Figure 8 ‣ 4.3 General Performance ‣ 4 Experiment ‣ Safe-LLaVA: A Privacy-Preserving Vision-Language Dataset and Benchmark for Biometric Safety" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>, we demonstrate that our safety filtering does not compromise semantic understanding, and that the model retains its general-purpose capabilities.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F8" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.00192v1/Effectiveness_of_Safe_LLaVA.png" id="S4.F8.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:405/241;" width="405" height="241" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 8: </span>Effectiveness of Safe-LLaVA-7B on both PRISM and General Benchmarks.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5 </span>Discussion</h2>

<div id="S5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this work, we addressed the challenge of biometric privacy in Vision-Language Models (VLMs) through two core contributions: (1) constructing a privacy-preserving dataset, and (2) introducing a benchmark for privacy-aware evaluation. First, we developed the <span id="S5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Safe-LLaVA</span> dataset by systematically removing biometric attributes such as eye color, gender, age, race, and body type—while preserving semantic content. Models trained on <span id="S5.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Safe-LLaVA</span> significantly reduced biometric leakage without compromising general performance, demonstrating the effectiveness of proactive dataset cleaning beyond existing memorization-focused approaches. Second, we proposed <span id="S5.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">PRISM</span>, the first benchmark explicitly designed to assess biometric privacy in VLMs. <span id="S5.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">PRISM</span> evaluates both refusal behavior on direct biometric queries and implicit leakage in open-ended responses. Our experiments show that <span id="S5.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Safe-LLaVA</span>-trained models achieve higher refusal accuracy and implicit leakage protection, validating the effectiveness of our <span id="S5.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Safe-LLaVA</span> dataset.</p>
</div>
<div class="ltx_pagination ltx_role_newpage"></div>
</section>
