<div id="Ax1.I1.i5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Ax1.I1.i5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Section <a href="#A6" title="Appendix F Experiment Statistical Significance ‣ Safe-LLaVA: A Privacy-Preserving Vision-Language Dataset and Benchmark for Biometric Safety" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">F</span></a>: Experiment Statistical Significance</p>
</div></li>
<li id="Ax1.I1.i6" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="Ax1.I1.i6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Ax1.I1.i6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Section <a href="#A7" title="Appendix G Reproduction of Supplementary ‣ Safe-LLaVA: A Privacy-Preserving Vision-Language Dataset and Benchmark for Biometric Safety" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">G</span></a>: Reproduction of Supplementary</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A1" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix A </span>Data, Code and Licenses</h2>

<section id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph"><span id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Safe-LLaVA</span> Dataset and Model License:</h5>

<div id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Safe-LLaVA (0.5B) and Safe-LLaVA (7B) share the same architecture as LLaVA-OneVision (0.5B) and LLaVA-v1.5 (7B), respectively, both of which are licensed under the Apache License 2.0<span id="footnote1" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup>
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">1</span>
              
              
              
            <a href="https://github.com/haotian-liu/LLaVA/blob/main/LICENSE" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_url ltx_font_typewriter">https://github.com/haotian-liu/LLaVA/blob/main/LICENSE</a></span></span></span>. Accordingly, the Safe-LLaVA models inherit the same license, permitting commercial use, modification, and redistribution with proper attribution and inclusion of the license notice. The <span id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Safe-LLaVA</span> dataset is a privacy-preserving derivative of the original LLaVA dataset, constructed by systematically removing biometric information while preserving semantic content. As a cleaned version of LLaVA, it is also released under the same Apache License 2.0.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">PRISM-Small Benchmark</h5>

<div id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Image data was scraped from publicly accessible websites. The usage of this content is compliant with fair-dealing law for non-commercial academic research. We do not redistribute the original images under commercial licensing.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A2" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix B </span>Implementation Details</h2>

<div id="A2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We pre-train the models on 2 NVIDIA A100 80GB GPUs and fine-tune on 4 A100 GPUs. The batch size for pre-trained and fine-tuning is 64 and 48, respectively.
For pretraining, we use the following hyperparameters: a learning rate of 1e-3, no weight decay, and a cosine learning rate scheduler with a warmup ratio of 0.03.
For fine-tuning, we lower the learning rate to 2e-5 while keeping the other configurations identical.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">All evaluations on PRISM benchmarks were conducted on a workstation equipped with two Intel Xeon Gold 5218 CPUs, each with 16 cores. The system also featured an NVIDIA TITAN RTX GPU with 24GB of memory.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Safe-LLaVA (0.5B) shares the same model architecture and training configuration as LLaVA-OneVision (0.5B)<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">30</a>]</cite>, and Safe-LLaVA (7B) is identical in architecture and setup to LLaVA-v1.5 (7B)<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">39</a>]</cite>. Both Safe-LLaVA (0.5B) and Safe-LLaVA (7B) are trained on the proposed <span id="A2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Safe-LLaVA</span> dataset using the exact same model settings. The only difference between baseline LLaVA-v1.5 (7B) and Safe-LLaVA (7B) lies in the training data: Safe-LLaVA models are trained on privacy-filtered corpora in which explicit and implicit biometric attributes have been removed.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A3" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix C </span><span id="A3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">PRISM-Large</span> Results</h2>

<div id="A3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Table <a href="#A3.T4" title="Table 4 ‣ Appendix C PRISM-Large Results ‣ Safe-LLaVA: A Privacy-Preserving Vision-Language Dataset and Benchmark for Biometric Safety" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> demonstrates refusal accuracy on <span id="A3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">PRISM-Large</span> across five biometric attributes, evaluated separately under soft and hard prompts. Across both evaluators (GPT-4 and Gemini-2.0-flash), baseline models such as Gemma, Qwen2.5-VL, and LLaVA variants exhibit poor refusal rates, often close to zero, with moderate improvements in larger-scale variants (e.g., LLaVA-v1.5 7B). In contrast, Safe-LLaVA achieves near-perfect or perfect refusal accuracy in almost all categories, demonstrating robustness under both prompt types. Notably, Safe-LLaVA (0.5B) consistently attains 100% refusal across attributes, while Safe-LLaVA (7B) also sustains strong performance with minor variations in gender refusal. These results highlight that Safe-LLaVA’s improvements are not only significant but also demonstrate strong generalizability, as the model maintains robust refusal accuracy even under the more diverse and large-scale settings of <span id="A3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">PRISM-Large</span>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In Table <a href="#A3.T5" title="Table 5 ‣ Appendix C PRISM-Large Results ‣ Safe-LLaVA: A Privacy-Preserving Vision-Language Dataset and Benchmark for Biometric Safety" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>, we present implicit leakage protection scores at the attribute level under open-ended queries. Baseline models show considerable leakage, with average protection scores ranging between <math id="A3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="58\textendash 89\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>58</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>–</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>89</mn></mrow><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">58\textendash 89\%</annotation></semantics></math>. Even stronger baselines (Qwen2.5-VL, LLaVA-v1.5) reveal vulnerability, particularly in sensitive categories such as gender. In stark contrast, Safe-LLaVA substantially outperforms all baselines, reaching average scores above <math id="A3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="99\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>99</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">99\%</annotation></semantics></math> for both 0.5B and 7B variants. The improvements are especially pronounced in gender leakage protection, where Safe-LLaVA surpasses baselines by more than <math id="A3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="70\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>70</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">70\%</annotation></semantics></math>. This demonstrates Safe-LLaVA’s capability to generalize protection effectively across all biometric attributes, substantially reducing unintended information disclosure.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A3.T4" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 4: </span>Refusal accuracy on the <span id="A3.T4.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">PRISM-Large</span> benchmark for each biometric attribute category, reported separately for soft(top) and hard(bottom) prompts. <span id="A3.T4.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Bold</span> indicates the best performance for each prompt type, while <span id="A3.T4.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#FF0000;">red</span> indicates the worst. * indicates base models trained under same settings as Safe-LLaVA.</figcaption>
